# modules/dast/active/checks.rules - the DAST active-tier script-check registry
# (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5; docs/STEP5-DAST-PLAN.md tiers 3 and 4). It carries
# the §7.2 safe-active content-discovery checks (DAST-DISC-*) and the §7.3
# active-injection checks (DAST-INJ-*).
#
# This is the first checks.rules the DAST module ships: auth.sh and crawl.sh
# cover no check (they acquire a session and build the inventory), so this file
# registers the first DAST checks tension 12 computes coverage over and
# tension 15's filter chain filters. Every record here is a `script` check, not
# a pattern - it names the shell script that implements it and carries
# `coverage-scope: target`, DAST's cell (the config/scope.conf target id,
# §9.5.1). checks_registry_load globs every *.rules under modules/dast/, so a
# later probe's records may live in a sibling file or here; the linter enforces
# one owning module (DAST) for all of them. Only a file named `checks.rules`
# takes the script-check schema (lib/records.sh's path table), which is why the
# §7.2 and §7.3 checks share this one file rather than each getting its own.
#
# The TYPE TAG is a per-check floor that must match the phase table's own tier
# (modules/dast/engine.sh). The §7.2 discovery checks are tagged `safe-active`,
# matching `active/discovery.sh:safe`, so they run at `--intensity safe` and up;
# the §7.3 SQLi checks are tagged `active`, matching `active/sqli.sh:active`, so
# they need `--intensity active`. Neither family is tagged `intrusive` - the
# discovery probes are read-only GETs and the injection payloads are read-only
# and non-destructive (docs/DESIGN.md §7.2/§7.3), so the intensity opt-in is the
# gate, and requiring --allow-intrusive on top would be a second, undocumented
# one. (The tests/fixtures/ copy tags an example SQLi check `intrusive` only to
# exercise that filter arm; it is not this shipped catalog.)
#
# One check id per technique, because the DAST fingerprint (target, method,
# path_template, param_location, param_name) does not carry the technique: a
# single id would make an error-based and a time-based finding on the same
# parameter collide and dedupe to one. rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.1.1 NAME allows
# `_`, so the technique lives in NAME under the shared `INJ` family.

id: DAST-INJ-SQLI_ERROR-01
title: SQL injection (error-based) via request parameter
script: active/sqli.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-89
owasp: A03:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Use parameterised queries (prepared statements) or an ORM that
  binds request-derived values, so a request parameter is never interpolated
  into SQL text. Return a generic error so a driver message never reaches the
  client, and run under a least-privilege database account.
references: CWE-89
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

id: DAST-INJ-SQLI_BOOLEAN-01
title: SQL injection (boolean-based) via request parameter
script: active/sqli.sh
severity: critical
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-89
owasp: A03:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Use parameterised queries (prepared statements) or an ORM that
  binds request-derived values, so a request parameter is never interpolated
  into SQL text. Add server-side input validation and run under a
  least-privilege database account.
references: CWE-89
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

id: DAST-INJ-SQLI_TIME-01
title: SQL injection (time-based blind) via request parameter
script: active/sqli.sh
severity: critical
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-89
owasp: A03:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Use parameterised queries (prepared statements) or an ORM that
  binds request-derived values, so a request parameter is never interpolated
  into SQL text. Add server-side input validation and run under a
  least-privilege database account.
references: CWE-89
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

id: DAST-DISC-SENSITIVE-01
title: Sensitive file or version-control metadata is web-accessible
script: active/discovery.sh
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-538
owasp: A05:2021
tags: safe-active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Remove the file from the web root, or block it at the server/CDN
  (deny access to dotfiles, version-control directories, config and dump
  files). Rotate any credential that may have been exposed, and add a
  deployment check that fails when such a file is reachable.
references: CWE-538
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/

id: DAST-DISC-BACKUP-01
title: Backup or temporary file is web-accessible
script: active/discovery.sh
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-530
owasp: A05:2021
tags: safe-active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Delete backup and editor temporary files from the web root and
  stop deployments from copying them there; block the known suffixes (.bak, ~,
  .old, .orig, .swp, ...) at the server or CDN, and rotate any secret the file
  may have disclosed.
references: CWE-530
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/

id: DAST-DISC-CONTENT-01
title: Undisclosed resource discovered via content discovery
script: active/discovery.sh
severity: low
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-200
owasp: A05:2021
tags: safe-active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Confirm the resource is meant to be public; if not, remove it or
  move it behind authentication. Ensure every reachable endpoint enforces its
  own access control rather than relying on being unlinked.
references: CWE-200
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/

id: DAST-DISC-DIRLIST-01
title: Directory listing is enabled
script: active/discovery.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-548
owasp: A05:2021
tags: safe-active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Disable automatic directory indexing (Apache `Options -Indexes`,
  nginx `autoindex off`, or the equivalent) and serve an explicit index or a
  403/404 so directory contents are never enumerated to clients.
references: CWE-548
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/

# --- DAST-15: reflected XSS (docs/DESIGN.md §7.3, `active/xss.sh`) -----------
#
# Three ids for the same defect class for the SAME reason sqli has three above:
# the DAST location profile carries no component naming the CONTEXT a
# reflection landed in, so a single id would make an HTML-text and an
# attribute-value reflection on one parameter collide and dedupe to one. The
# context is what decides whether a given raw character is dangerous - a raw
# `"` is harmless in element text and a break-out inside a quoted attribute -
# so it is the thing a reader needs and the thing the remediation differs on.
#
# `tags: active`, matching the phase table's `active/xss.sh:active` floor. NOT
# `intrusive`: the probe sends one unique alphanumeric marker with `<`, `>`,
# `"` and `'` appended and nothing else - no script, no event handler, no
# `javascript:` URL, nothing that executes - so the value is inert wherever it
# lands and the active-intensity opt-in is the right and only gate.

id: DAST-INJ-XSS_REFLECTED_HTML-01
title: Reflected cross-site scripting - unescaped reflection in HTML text
script: active/xss.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-79
owasp: A03:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: HTML-entity-encode request-derived data where it is written into
  element text, at the point of output rather than on input. Prefer a template
  engine that escapes by default and do not disable it for this value. Add a
  Content-Security-Policy without unsafe-inline as defence in depth.
references: CWE-79
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

id: DAST-INJ-XSS_REFLECTED_ATTR-01
title: Reflected cross-site scripting - unescaped reflection in an HTML attribute value
script: active/xss.sh
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-79
owasp: A03:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Quote every attribute value and encode the quote character that
  delimits it (and `<`, `>`, `&`) when writing request-derived data into an
  attribute. Never build an attribute value by string concatenation in a
  template, and never place request-derived data in an event-handler or a URL
  attribute without a scheme allow-list.
references: CWE-79
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

id: DAST-INJ-XSS_REFLECTED_JS-01
title: Reflected cross-site scripting - unescaped reflection in a script block
script: active/xss.sh
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-79
owasp: A03:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Do not interpolate request-derived data into JavaScript source.
  Pass it to the page as data - a JSON payload the script reads, or a data-
  attribute - and encode it for that carrier, so `<`, `>` and both quote
  characters can never terminate a string literal or the script element itself.
references: CWE-79
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/
